@startuml oracle_fidelity
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam dpi 150
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam noteBorderColor #DDDDDD

title RQ2 — Does each signal track the causal oracle E(t)?\n(ILLUSTRATIVE: Spearman of method importance vs. true token-ablation effect)

note as M
|= localization signal |= rho with oracle |=  bar (0 ............ 1)  |
|= Accumulated attention | 0.55 | <color:#1a9850>**###########**</color><color:#D9D9D9>#########</color> |
|= QRHead attention      | 0.46 | <color:#66bd63>**#########**</color><color:#D9D9D9>###########</color> |
|= Wu attention          | 0.44 | <color:#a6d96a>**#########**</color><color:#D9D9D9>###########</color> |
|= Transcoder attr       | 0.33 | <color:#fdae61>**#######**</color><color:#D9D9D9>#############</color> |
end note

legend bottom
Read as a *result shape*, not numbers. A signal can look authoritative on its own task yet track the
causal oracle only weakly — and the four disagree about which tokens matter (see the agreement matrix).
The estimator itself is validated separately by gate 11.2a: Spearman(E_hat, E) >= 0.8 with high sign-agreement.
endlegend

@enduml
